<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02HP">Notation <span data-tag="02HP">4.7.3.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an $\infty $-category. We will sometimes use the symbol ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ to denote a final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, provided that such an object exists. In this case, we will sometimes abuse terminology by referring to ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ as <em>the</em> final object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. This abuse is justified by Corollary <a data-tag="02HM" href="/tag/02HM">4.7.3.14</a>, which guarantees that ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ is uniquely determined up to a contractible space of choices (in particular, it is well-defined up to isomorphism). Similarly, we will often use the symbol $\emptyset _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ to denote an initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ (if such an object exists), and refer to it as <em>the</em> initial object of $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$. When it is unlikely to cause confusion, we might omit the subscripts and denote the objects $\emptyset _{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ and ${\bf 1}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ by $\emptyset $ and ${\bf 1}$, respectively. </p>
